  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Eley (essay originally published in Calhoun's 1992
  volume; cited here in its 1994 anthology reprint) argues against the
  existence of any single, coherent bourgeois public sphere in
  nineteenth-century history. Redefining the public sphere as "the
  structured setting where cultural and ideological contest or
