  Domain 1), reinforcing the review's case that Habermas's original
  historical narrative understates a plurality of contesting publics
  present from the outset.

  POSITION: Pluralist/historicist critique of the singular bourgeois
  public sphere thesis, allied with but analytically distinct from
  Fraser's normative feminist critique.
  },
  keywords = {public-sphere, historical-critique, pluralism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{landes1988women,
  author = {Landes, Joan B.},
  title = {Women and the Public Sphere in the Age of the French Revolution},
  publisher = {Cornell University Press},
  year = {1988},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Landes argues that the bourgeois public sphere was
  constituted through, not merely alongside, the exclusion of women.
  Tracing the transition from the Old Regime's salon culture — in which
  aristocratic women exercised real political and cultural influence — to
  the revolutionary and post-revolutionary public sphere, she shows how
  Enlightenment appeals to universal reason and nature were built on an
  ideologically sanctioned gender division between public (masculine,
  rational) and private (feminine, domestic) spheres, culminating in
  republican ideals that confined women to a passive, symbolic
  "republican motherhood" role even as revolutionary rhetoric proclaimed
  universal rights.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the historical case study underlying the feminist
  critique of Habermas's account: the bourgeois public sphere's claim to
  formal inclusivity and gender-neutral rational-critical discourse is
  shown to be historically false rather than merely incompletely
  realized, strengthening the review's argument that exclusion was
  constitutive rather than incidental.

  POSITION: Feminist historical critique; a foundational reference
  alongside Fraser (Domain 1) for the "constitutive exclusion" line of
  argument against the bourgeois public sphere model.
  },
  keywords = {public-sphere, feminist-critique, gender, exclusion, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{warner2002publics,
  author = {Warner, Michael},
  title = {Publics and Counterpublics},
  journal = {Public Culture},
  year = {2002},
  volume = {14},
  number = {1},
  pages = {49--90},
  doi = {10.1215/08992363-14-1-49},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Warner develops a general theory of "publics" as
  self-organizing social entities constituted reflexively through address
  and circulation of discourse, rather than as pre-given groups. Building
  on and generalizing Fraser's concept of "subaltern counterpublics," he
  analyzes how counterpublics (e.g., queer publics) differ from dominant
  publics not merely by holding different views but by having a
  structurally marked, oppositional relation to the norms of the
  dominant public — differing in tone, idiom, and stance toward the
  wider social world.

  RELEVANCE: Extends the Fraser-initiated counterpublics vocabulary
  (Domain 1) into a stand-alone theoretical framework used across later
  literature (including this domain's digital-counterpublics entries), so
  the review can trace a direct conceptual line from Habermas's single
  public sphere through Fraser's subaltern counterpublics to Warner's
  fully pluralized theory of publics.

  POSITION: Post-Habermasian pluralist theory of publics; treats the
  Habermasian rational-critical public as one historically specific
  public-formation among many, not the master case.
  },
  keywords = {public-sphere, counterpublics, queer-theory, High},
  abstract = {By combining historical analysis, theoretical reflection, and extended case studies, Publics and Counterpublics shows how the idea of a public works as a formal device in modern culture and traces its implications for contemporary life. Michael Warner offers a revisionist account at the junction of two intellectual traditions with which he has been associated: public-sphere theory and queer theory. To public-sphere theory, this book brings a new emphasis on cultural forms, and a new focus on the dynamics of counterpublics. To queer theory, it brings a new way of seeing how queer culture (among other examples) is shaped by the counter-public environment. (Description of Warner's book-length elaboration of the same essay/argument, Zone Books, 2002.)},
  abstract_source = {core}
}

