  year = {2022},
  volume = {39},
  number = {4},
  pages = {129--143},
  doi = {10.1177/02632764221103527},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Staab and Thiel argue for returning to political-economic
  fundamentals to analyze the contemporary public sphere, identifying a
  characteristic connection in social media platforms between
  digital-behavioral control and "singularized" audiences operating
  within proprietary markets. They contend that platforms politically
