  commercial interests retain primacy — a condition they term
  "privatization without privatism," distinct from the passive
  consumerism Habermas associated with mass-media refeudalization.

  RELEVANCE: Directly updates Habermas's "structural transformation"
  and "refeudalization" theses (Domain 1's primary texts) for the
  platform era, giving the review a contemporary political-economy
  counterpart to the historical feminist/pluralist critiques.

  POSITION: Political-economy revision of the structural-transformation
  thesis; treats platformization as a genuinely new transformation rather
  than a simple repetition of mass-media refeudalization.
  },
  keywords = {public-sphere, digital-public-sphere, platform-capitalism, High},
  abstract = {This article explores the question of how to understand social media following the Habermasian theory of the structural transformation of the public sphere. We argue for a return to political-economic fundamentals as the basis for analysing the public sphere and seek to establish a characteristic connection between digital-behavioural control and singularised audiences in the context of proprietary markets. In the digital constellation, it is less a matter of immobilising the citizen as a consumer but rather of their political activation – albeit in conditions under which commercial interests have primacy: privatisation without privatism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{seeligersevignani2022new,
